@vijayranganathan8580  I think this needs to be looked at beyond movies and emotional reactions. Over the last 15 years, Vijay did not just act in films he systematically built Vijay Makkal Iyakkam with cadres who are 18+ and continuously attracting upcoming first-time voters. Youth and new voters are a major strength in the current and future elections, especially when Tamil Nadu’s youngsters have been politically disengaged or disillusioned due to decades of corruption and stagnant Dravidian politics.
He consistently raised his voice on real issues—corruption, failures in the current system, fishermen’s livelihood, NEET, Jallikattu, and the Thoothukudi firing—when many remained silent. Political change doesn’t always start with announcing “top three promises”; sometimes it starts by building awareness, cadres, and discipline at the grassroots.
Revolutions are not created overnight. Booth-level organization, youth mobilization, and issue-based politics take time. Dismissing all this as “hallucination” ignores ground realities and the slow but deliberate groundwork that has been laid for meaningful change, whether in this election or the future
